 rows=[('Product Trio and empowered teams','Confirmed Agile product development with product and design counterparts. At Dalux, align scope and trade-offs with the Product Manager, Product Designer, Tech Lead and developers.'),('Value, usability and feasibility','Data Power: technical applications from customer needs to integration and handover. Propose reviewing value and usability with product and design, and feasibility with the technical team.'),('Planning and quality delivery','Novo: 37 projects, 25 specialists and zero critical deviations at handover. Agree capacity, dependencies, risks and quality expectations before making commitments.'),('Continuous improvement and developer experience','Data Power: AI-assisted development and review/test standards. Listen to developers and test one improvement against a clear outcome without sacrificing quality.'),('Cross-team initiatives and leadership forums','Novo: coordinated engineering, procurement and site stakeholders across projects. Share decisions, dependencies and lessons with other Dalux teams and leadership forums.'),('Coaching, 1:1s and career development','User confirms developer coaching, mentoring, regular 1:1s and career discussions. ELOHVAC: daily leadership, 1:1s and training for eight people.'),('Recruitment, placement and contracts','User confirms personally conducting developer recruitment interviews, assessing placement and finalising employment contracts. Employer, dates and specific cases not provided.'),('Performance and salary','User confirms handling developer performance improvement and annual salary adjustments. Follow Dalux policies and apply clear, fair expectations; specific cases not provided.'),('Technical leadership and software background','Data Power: led a 4–10-person AI department and AI-assisted development. Background is principally technical leadership rather than conventional hands-on software development; stack-specific expertise is not claimed.'),('University degree or similar','B.Tech in Technology Management and Mechanical Engineering; not a Computer Science or Information Technology degree. Technical management and industrial experience provide an adjacent foundation.')]
